# `{{` IS ALREADY SPOKEN FOR, AND THIS FILE MEASURES ITS OWN LIMITS.
#
# `law.brace` binds braces to structured packs, descriptor homes and descriptor
# application; `law.literal.text` binds interpolation to literal AND EXPRESSION
# segments. An expression may begin with `{`, so `{{` already spells "a hole
# whose expression begins with a pack". Python, C#, Rust, Zig and C++
# `std::format` spend `{{` on a literal brace; the ruled spelling here is `\{`,
# which `c` and `d` measure live.
#
# THE `{{` ROW IS A RECORDED LIMIT, NOT A CLAIM. This file used to declare
# `n=3 spaced=3` and produced neither: `print("n={{10, 20, 30}:len()}")` is
# refused whole, on `--backend=c` and `--backend=wasm` alike, at
#
#   concatOperand other: kind=method_call
#   lowerBinop refuse op=concat lhs_kind=quoted rhs_kind=method_call
#   hint: refused at: binop-not-lowered:concat
#
# The `method_call` operand is the measurement. Under a `{{`-as-escape reading
# that literal holds no application and there is nothing to refuse — it would
# answer the 21 bytes `c` answers. So the obligation is both halves: `c` stays
# 21 AND that spelling stays refused. The day one answer serves both, `{{` has
# been taken.
#
# IT ALSO DECLARED `pack=3` FOR `{1, 2, 3}:len()`. The two pack faces disagree:
# `p.len` is 3 and `p:len()` is 0, both at exit 0, on both realizations. `e` is
# the face that answers and `f` is the control that the pack is not empty;
# `gaps/GAP-241.md` owns the face that does not. This file does not pin the 0.
#
# `a` is the premise the ruling rests on — a hole carries an APPLICATION and
# not just a name: 2, the length of `bc`, where its own source is 13 bytes.
# `g` is the same expression spaced away from the delimiters: if `a` and `g`
# ever part, whitespace has become semantic inside a hole. `b` is the control
# that ruling on `\{` did not disable ordinary holes.
# expect: 2 2 3 21 1 3 20
# @corpus current

s = "abcdef"
x = 7
p = {10, 20, 30}

a = "{s:sub(2, 3)}":len()
g = "{ s:sub(2, 3) }":len()
b = "v={x}":len()
c = "n=\{10, 20, 30}:len()}":len()
d = "\{":len()
e = p.len
f = p[2]

print("{a} {g} {b} {c} {d} {e} {f}")
0
